$CATEGORY: Physical Education and Health/Unit 1/Lesson 1

::U1L1-01::Which statement best defines wellness? {=An ongoing process of informed choices and use of supports to maintain or improve health ~A fixed score based only on physical condition ~The absence of every illness or challenge ~A measure of athletic performance}

::U1L1-02::Which dimension includes sleep, movement, hydration, and recovery? {=Physical health ~Mental health ~Social health ~Environmental health}

::U1L1-03::Which dimension most directly includes concentration, learning, and problem-solving? {=Mental health ~Physical health ~Social health ~Environmental health}

::U1L1-04::Which dimension includes recognizing and managing feelings? {=Emotional health ~Environmental health ~Physical health ~Social health}

::U1L1-05::Which dimension includes relationships, communication, boundaries, and belonging? {=Social health ~Mental health ~Physical health ~Environmental health}

::U1L1-06::Which is an environmental-health factor? {=Poor lighting on a walking route ~A student's skill at solving equations ~A disagreement between two friends ~A person's ability to name an emotion}

::U1L1-07::What is a protective factor? {=A condition, skill, relationship, or resource that can reduce risk or support health ~A guarantee that a negative outcome cannot occur ~Any factor outside a person's control ~A physical characteristic that proves wellness}

::U1L1-08::What is a risk factor? {=A condition or behavior that can increase the likelihood of harm ~A medical diagnosis made from a short scenario ~A resource that always improves every health dimension ~A measurement of athletic ability}

::U1L1-09::Which statement about health status is most accurate? {=It should be considered using multiple kinds of information across dimensions ~It can be determined from appearance alone ~It never changes once established ~It depends only on personal choices}

::U1L1-10::Why are the wellness dimensions shown as interconnected? {=A behavior or circumstance in one dimension may influence other dimensions ~Every change produces the same outcome for every person ~Physical health always controls the other dimensions ~The dimensions must be scored from strongest to weakest}

::U1L1-11::Lena sleeps poorly and then has trouble focusing during class. Which interaction is most directly supported? {=Physical health may influence mental health ~Social health guarantees physical health ~Environmental health proves emotional health ~Mental health determines appearance}

::U1L1-12::A student begins walking with a friend and enjoys both the movement and conversation. Which dimensions are most directly involved? {=Physical and social ~Mental and environmental only ~Emotional and environmental only ~Physical only}

::U1L1-13::A noisy study space makes it difficult for a student to follow instructions. Which interaction is best supported? {=Environmental conditions may affect mental focus ~Social health determines the noise level ~Physical appearance affects learning ~Emotional health guarantees concentration}

::U1L1-14::A student sees edited fitness posts and begins believing appearance proves health. What is the relevant influence? {=Media influence ~Heredity only ~Hydration ~Physical recovery}

::U1L1-15::Which response uses appropriate evidence-based language? {=The late schedule may contribute to tiredness and weaker concentration ~The schedule definitely caused a medical condition ~The student is unhealthy because of appearance ~Everyone will respond to the schedule in the same way}

::U1L1-16::A route has poor lighting and no sidewalk. What is the most responsible response? {=Choose a safer alternative or time and seek help identifying a safe route ~Use the route because completing activity matters more than safety ~Assume the conditions will not matter ~Increase the activity intensity to finish faster}

::U1L1-17::Which is the clearest example of a protective factor? {=A trusted adult helps a student identify realistic options ~Repeated insufficient sleep ~An unsafe walking route ~Unmanaged schedule pressure}

::U1L1-18::Which is the clearest example of a risk factor? {=Repeatedly sleeping too little before school ~Access to a refill station ~A predictable break at work ~A supportive friend}

::U1L1-19::Which action could support both physical and mental dimensions for a tired student? {=Plan a consistent sleep routine and ask for support if difficulty continues ~Judge health from appearance ~Ignore the evidence and add more commitments ~Diagnose the cause without qualified help}

::U1L1-20::Which analysis is strongest? {=The quieter space is an environmental change that may support mental focus because the student reports following directions more easily ~The student is healthy because the room is quiet ~The room caused every change in the student's life ~Environmental health and mental health are unrelated}

::U1L1-21::Which factor may require adaptation or outside support rather than immediate personal control? {=Unsafe neighborhood conditions ~Choosing to ask a trusted adult for help ~Preparing water before activity ~Limiting an unreliable media account}

::U1L1-22::Why can seeking appropriate help demonstrate personal responsibility? {=It uses available resources to respond safely to a need or barrier ~It transfers every health decision to someone else ~It guarantees a perfect outcome ~It proves the person cannot influence any behavior}

::U1L1-23::A student feels disconnected after changing schools and receives information about an interest group. The interest group is best classified as what? {=A possible social protective factor ~A physical risk factor ~Proof of a diagnosis ~A measure of health status based on appearance}

::U1L1-24::Which recommendation is most appropriate for a student whose busy schedule leads to skipped breaks and late work? {=Review commitments, use available breaks, and create a realistic schedule ~Add more commitments to build endurance ~Ignore tiredness because health is fixed ~Judge the student's wellness from body size}

::U1L1-25::Which conclusion best reflects the lesson? {=Overall wellness involves interacting dimensions, evidence-based choices, adaptation, and appropriate support ~Health is determined only by exercise and nutrition ~A challenge in one dimension means a person failed at wellness ~One health indicator is enough to define total wellness}
